  <p>
    Banking in Dubai comes with its own version of the new arrival catch-22: most banks
    require a UAE residence visa and Emirates ID to open a full account — which means you
    can't do it the moment you land. The good news is there are ways to get started quickly,
    and once your residency paperwork is sorted, the process is fairly straightforward.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--note">
    <span class="callout__heading">You need your Emirates ID first</span>
    <p>
      Most UAE banks will not open a full current account without a valid Emirates ID card.
      Sort your visa and Emirates ID first — see our <a href="/dubai/visas">Dubai Visas guide</a>.
      In the meantime, Wise gives you usable account details from day one.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Start before you arrive: Wise</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>Wise</strong> can be set up before you land in Dubai and gives you international
    account details including AED (UAE dirham) capability. It won't replace a full UAE bank
    account but it's excellent for receiving your first salary transfer, paying international
    bills, and converting currency at fair exchange rates.
  </p>

