  <h2>UAE banks: your main options</h2>

  <p>
    Once you have your Emirates ID, you can open a full UAE bank account. The main banks
    for new expat residents are:
  </p>

  <h3>Emirates NBD</h3>
  <p>
    The UAE's largest bank by assets, with branches everywhere in Dubai. Offers a full range
    of accounts including salary accounts, savings and investments. Their digital bank
    <strong>Liv.</strong> (below) is worth considering for a simpler setup.
  </p>

  <h3>First Abu Dhabi Bank (FAB)</h3>
  <p>
    One of the UAE's largest banks, well-regarded for expats. Good international banking
    capabilities and competitive rates.
  </p>

  <h3>Mashreq</h3>
  <p>
    Known for being relatively easy to work with for new residents and having a decent
    digital banking experience.
  </p>

  <h3>RAK Bank</h3>
  <p>
    Often recommended by expats for its straightforward account opening process, good
    customer service and no-minimum-balance options.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--tip">
    <span class="callout__heading">✦ Salary account</span>
    <p>
      Many UAE employment contracts specify that your salary must be paid into a particular
      bank (often Emirates NBD or FAB). Check your contract before choosing — your employer's
      WPS (Wage Protection System) integration may determine which bank you open with first.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Liv. — the digital option</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>Liv.</strong> (by Emirates NBD) is Dubai's most popular digital-first bank for
    young professionals and expats. It's app-based, quick to set up once you have your
    Emirates ID, offers good features including budgeting tools, no minimum balance
    requirements on the basic account, and a Mastercard debit card.
  </p>

