  <h2>What you'll need to open an account</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Valid Emirates ID</strong> — essential for all banks</li>
    <li><strong>Passport</strong> — with valid UAE residence visa</li>
    <li><strong>Employment letter</strong> — confirming your job, salary and employer (most banks require this)</li>
    <li><strong>Salary slips</strong> — usually 3 months (or a new starter letter if you've just arrived)</li>
    <li><strong>UAE phone number</strong> — for OTP verification and account management</li>
    <li><strong>Proof of address</strong> — tenancy contract or Ejari certificate once you have accommodation</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Key differences from UK banking</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>No credit score system</strong> — the UAE has its own credit bureau (Al Etihad Credit Bureau) but it works differently from UK credit scores. Building a UAE credit history takes time, and it starts from zero when you arrive.</li>
    <li><strong>Cheque culture</strong> — you'll likely need to issue post-dated cheques for rent. Most UAE banks provide a cheque book; request one when you open your account.</li>
    <li><strong>Minimum balances</strong> — many traditional UAE bank accounts have minimum balance requirements (often AED 3,000–5,000) to avoid fees. Read the terms carefully. Liv. and some RAK Bank accounts have no minimum balance.</li>
    <li><strong>No FSCS equivalent</strong> — UAE bank deposits are protected up to AED 500,000 per person under the UAE Banks Deposit Protection Scheme.</li>
    <li><strong>International transfers</strong> — Wise is significantly cheaper than using UAE banks for sending money internationally. Set up Wise for transfers even if you use a UAE bank as your main account.</li>
  </ul>

